Project Title

Titanic Survival Prediction Using Machine Learning

Description

The objective of this project is to analyze the Titanic passenger dataset and build machine learning models to predict whether a passenger survived or not. The project involves data preprocessing, exploratory data analysis (EDA), visualization, and model building using classification algorithms. By applying machine learning techniques, the project aims to identify important factors that influenced survival during the Titanic disaster and evaluate the performance of different models.

Dataset
Source: Kaggle Titanic Dataset
https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/titanic/data
Description:
The dataset contains information about passengers aboard the Titanic, including demographic and travel-related details. It consists of features such as passenger class, gender, age, fare, number of family members onboard, and embarkation port. The dataset includes a target variable called Survived, which indicates whether a passenger survived (1) or not (0). This structured dataset is widely used for classification problems in machine learning.
Steps Performed
Data Cleaning
Handled missing values in columns like Age, Cabin, and Embarked
Removed irrelevant features such as Name, Ticket, and Cabin (if needed)
Converted categorical variables into numerical format using encoding
Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)
Examined dataset structure and data types
Identified missing values and outliers
Analyzed relationships between features and survival
Visualization
Created histograms to understand data distribution
Used bar charts to compare survival rates across categories
Generated box plots to detect outliers
Plotted correlation heatmap to understand feature relationships
Model Building
Implemented K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN)
Applied Logistic Regression
Built Decision Tree Classifier
Trained models and evaluated performance using accuracy
Results
Key Findings:
Female passengers had a higher survival rate than males
Passengers in higher classes (1st class) had better survival chances
Age and fare also influenced survival probability
Family size had some impact on survival
Metrics:
Accuracy was used as the primary evaluation metric
Logistic Regression and Decision Tree showed good performance
KNN performance depended on the value of K
Tools Used
Programming Language: Python
Libraries:
NumPy
Pandas
Matplotlib
Seaborn
Scikit-learn
Conclusion

This project demonstrated how machine learning techniques can be applied to predict outcomes using real-world data. By performing exploratory data analysis and applying classification algorithms, meaningful insights were obtained about the factors influencing survival in the Titanic dataset. The comparison of different models helped in understanding their strengths and limitations. Overall, the project highlights the importance of data preprocessing, feature selection, and model evaluation in machine learning workflows.

